The speed of various ADC types in <strong>increasing order</strong> is: \[\underbrace{\text{Dual slope (Integrating)}}_{\text{Slowest}}
&lt; \text{Counting (Counter) type}
&lt; \text{Successive approximation}
&lt; \underbrace{\text{Parallel comparator (Flash)}}_{\text{Fastest}}\] The <strong>integrating type</strong> (dual slope) ADC is the slowest because it requires charging and discharging of a capacitor over a fixed time period. It needs \(2^{n+1}\) clock pulses for an \(n\)-bit conversion. (C) Integrating type <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Conversion time comparison:</strong><br/><ul><li>Flash ADC: \(1 \cdot T_{CLK}\) (fastest)</li><li>SAR ADC: \(n \cdot T_{CLK}\)</li><li>Counter type: \((2^n - 1) \cdot T_{CLK}\) (average)</li><li>Dual slope (Integrating): \(2^{n+1} \cdot T_{CLK}\) (slowest)</li></ul></li><li>Despite being the slowest, the dual slope ADC provides the <strong>highest accuracy</strong> and <strong>best noise immunity</strong> among all ADC types.</li></ul></div>

Given the characteristic equation: \(s^3 + 2s^2 + Ks + K = 0\) We can rewrite it in the standard open-loop form \(1 + G(s)H(s) = 0\): \[1 + \frac{K(s + 1)}{s^3 + 2s^2} = 0 \implies 1 + \frac{K(s + 1)}{s^2(s + 2)} = 0\] \[G(s)H(s) = \frac{K(s + 1)}{s^2(s + 2)}\] Here, the number of open-loop poles (\(P\)) is 3 (at \(s = 0, 0, -2\)), and the number of open-loop zeroes (\(Z\)) is 1 (at \(s = -1\)). The number of asymptotes is \(P - Z = 3 - 1 = 2\). <strong>1. Angle of Asymptotes and Real Axis Intercept</strong> \[\theta = \frac{(2k + 1) \times 180^\circ}{P - Z}, \quad \text{for } k = 0, 1\] \[= 90^\circ,270^\circ\] The real axis intercept (centroid, \(\sigma_A\)) is: \[\sigma_A = \frac{\sum \text{Re}(\text{poles}) - \sum \text{Re}(\text{zeroes})}{P - Z}\] \[= \frac{(0 + 0 - 2) - (-1)}{2} = -0.5\] <strong>2. Break-away / Break-in Points</strong> From the characteristic equation, express \(K\) in terms of \(s\): \[K = -\frac{s^2(s + 2)}{s + 1} = -\frac{s^3 + 2s^2}{s + 1}\] Differentiating \(K\) with respect to \(s\) and setting it to zero (\(\frac{dK}{ds} = 0\)): \[\frac{dK}{ds} = -\left[ \frac{(3s^2 + 4s)(s + 1) - (s^3 + 2s^2)}{(s + 1)^2} \right] = 0\] \[(3s^3 + 3s^2 + 4s^2 + 4s) - (s^3 + 2s^2) = 0\] \[2s^3 + 5s^2 + 4s = 0 \implies s(2s^2 + 5s + 4) = 0\] \[s = 0, s = -1.25 \pm j0.66\] Only \(s = 0\) is a valid breakaway point because it lies on the root locus path between the two open-loop poles located at the origin. <strong>3. Imaginary Axis Crossing Points</strong> Using the RH Array : \(s^3 + 2s^2 + Ks + K = 0\): \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; K \\
s^2 &amp; 2 &amp; K \\
s^1 &amp; \dfrac{K}{2}  \\
s^0 &amp; K &amp; 
\end{array}\] For the system to sustain oscillations (crossing the imaginary axis), the \(s^1\) row must be zero: \[\frac{K}{2} = 0 \implies K = 0\] Since \(K = 0\) corresponds to the starting points of the root locus (at the open-loop poles \(s = 0\)), there is <strong>no imaginary axis intersection point</strong> for any \(K &gt; 0\). <strong>4. Root Locus Sketch</strong><br/><img src="img/8a0d32eb4d254f83.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,420px)">

